{
    "id":  "HEPATOBILIARY_DIS_HB_009",
    "code":  "ALCOHOLIC_CIRRHOSIS_OF_LIVER_WITHOUT_ASCITES",
    "name":  "Alcoholic cirrhosis of liver without ascites",
    "module":  "hepatobiliary",
    "category":  "disease",
    "description":  "Alcoholic cirrhosis is the end-stage consequence of chronic alcohol-related liver disease, characterised by irreversible diffuse hepatic fibrosis and formation of regenerative nodules that distort the hepatic architecture and cause portal hypertension. At the compensated stage (without ascites), patients may be relatively asymptomatic or present with fatigue, jaundice, and stigmata of chronic liver disease (spider naevi, palmar erythema, gynaecomastia, Dupuytren\u0027s contracture, parotid enlargement). The transition to decompensated cirrhosis (ascites, variceal bleeding, hepatic encephalopathy, jaundice) is a major prognostic landmark.",
